{"name":"Pale Olive","hex":"#d3c7a1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Light in color.","Having a pallor (a light color, especially due to sickness, shock, fright etc.)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To turn pale; to lose colour.","To become insignificant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pale"},{"term":"olive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree of species Olea europaea cultivated since ancient times in the Mediterranean for its fruit and the oil obtained from it.","The small oval fruit of this tree, eaten ripe (usually black) or unripe (usually green)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish green color, that of an unripe olive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Olive","blocks":[{"text":"The cultivated olive is a subspecies of subtropical evergreen tree in the family Oleaceae. Modern cultivars are traced primarily to the Near East, Aegean Sea, and Strait of Gibraltar. Olea europaea is the type species for its genus, Olea, which lends its name to the Oleaceae plant family. The olive fruit is classed botanically as a drupe, similar in structure and function to the cherry or peach. The term oil was originally synonymous with olive oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Olivesfromjordan.jpg/330px-Olivesfromjordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive","wikidata":"Q1621080"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)","wikidata":"Q864152"},{"title":"Colorfulness","blocks":[{"text":"Colorfulness, chroma and saturation are attributes of perceived color relating to chromatic intensity. As defined formally by the International Commission on Illumination (CIE) they respectively describe three different aspects of chromatic intensity, but the terms are often used loosely and interchangeably in contexts where these aspects are not clearly distinguished. The precise meanings of the terms vary by what other functions they are dependent on."},{"items":["Colorfulness is the \"attribute of a visual perception according to which the perceived color of an area appears to be more or less chromatic \". The colorfulness evoked by an object depends not only on its spectral reflectance but also on the strength of the illumination, and increases with the latter unless the brightness is very high.","Chroma is the \"colorfulness of an area judged as a proportion of the brightness of a similarly illuminated area that appears white or highly transmitting\". As a result, chroma is mostly only dependent on the spectral properties, and as such is seen to describe the object color. It is how different from a grey of the same lightness such an object color appears to be.","Saturation is the \"colorfulness of an area judged in proportion to its brightness\", which in effect is the perceived freedom from whitishness of the light coming from the area. An object with a given spectral reflectance exhibits approximately constant saturation for all levels of illumination, unless the brightness is very high."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Brightness_and_colorfulness.png/330px-Brightness_and_colorfulness.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colorfulness","wikidata":"Q27673"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg/500px-Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg/1280px-Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg/500px-Oliveira_%28Olea_europaea%29_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_%288160_x_6120px%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOliveira_(Olea_europaea)_na_Pra%C3%A7a_da_Esta%C3%A7%C3%A3o%2C_Bag%C3%A9%2C_RS%2C_Brasil_(8160_x_6120px).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/ZeitimI.jpg/120px-ZeitimI.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/ZeitimI.jpg/250px-ZeitimI.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/ZeitimI.jpg/120px-ZeitimI.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZeitimI.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Olea_europaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-229.jpg/250px-Olea_europaea_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-229.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn